--- machine\software\pki
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki

Configuration of the pkid certificate authority service.

Administrators own these keys. pkid needs read and subtree-notify access and
has no configuration-write authority. Write access to any key here is
equivalent to administering the CA: it decides which certificates are issued
and to whom. Grant it only to principals you would trust to issue any
certificate. Every change is recorded as an event.

Each key's own security descriptor controls who may change it. Request and
enrolment rights are separate descriptors stored as values, so editing a
policy and being granted by it are never the same right.

--- machine\software\pki\settings
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Settings

Settings that apply to every CA on the machine.

--- machine\software\pki\settings sidsecurityextension
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Settings SidSecurityExtension
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
valid: 0 or 1
applies: new certificates

Whether certificates carry Microsoft's SID security extension
(1.3.6.1.4.1.311.25.2), which binds a certificate to the principal that owns
its names.

Keep this set to 1. The extension lets services that accept certificates for
sign-in match the certificate to an account by SID rather than by name, which
is what prevents certificates with look-alike names from signing in as
someone else. Turning it off removes that protection from every certificate
issued afterwards, and certificates already issued keep whatever they have.
The pki command and window do not offer this setting.

A certificate whose names have no single owner never carries the extension.

--- machine\software\pki\templates
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates

Certificate templates, one subkey per template, named by a GUID that never
changes. Renaming a template changes its Name value, not its key.

A template fixes everything about a certificate except its names and key.
Requesters can't override any of it.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> name
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> Name
type: REG_SZ
default: (required)
valid: non-empty text
applies: new requests

The template's display name, such as Server or Device.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> purposes
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> Purposes
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (required)
valid: one or more of server-auth, client-auth, code-signing, email-protection, time-stamping, ocsp-signing, or a dotted object identifier
applies: new requests

The extended key usages every certificate from this template carries.

At least one is required. The Any Purpose usage (2.5.29.37.0) is refused.
Templates never issue CA certificates: those are made only by the explicit
subordinate-CA operation, which requires Manage CA.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> keyusages
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> KeyUsages
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (required)
valid: one or more of digital-signature, content-commitment, key-encipherment, data-encipherment, key-agreement
applies: new requests

The key usages every certificate from this template carries. Certificate and
CRL signing can't be listed.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> lifetimedays
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> LifetimeDays
type: REG_DWORD
default: (required)
valid: 1–7305
applies: new certificates

How long certificates from this template are valid. A certificate never
outlives its issuing CA, so a CA close to expiry issues shorter certificates.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> renewalwindowdays
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> RenewalWindowDays
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: less than LifetimeDays
applies: new certificates

How many days before expiry renewal becomes due.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> keyalgorithms
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> KeyAlgorithms
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (required)
valid: one or more of ecdsa-p256, ecdsa-p384, rsa-N with N from 2048 to 8192
applies: new requests

The keys a request may contain. rsa-N accepts RSA keys of at least N bits.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> nametypes
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> NameTypes
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (required)
valid: one or more of dns, email, upn
applies: new requests

The kinds of name certificates from this template may carry. A request
naming anything else is refused. IP addresses are not covered by name policy
and are always refused.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> commonname
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> CommonName
type: REG_SZ
default: first-name
valid: none, first-name, owner-display-name
applies: new certificates

Where the certificate's common name comes from: nowhere, the first approved
name, or the display name of the principal that owns every requested name.
The display name comes from identity data, never from the request. A
request whose names have no single owner can't use owner-display-name.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> issuance
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> Issuance
type: REG_SZ
default: immediate
valid: immediate, approval
applies: new requests

Whether permitted requests are issued at once or always held for an
officer's approval.

--- machine\software\pki\templates\<guid> enrollsecurity
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Templates\<guid> EnrollSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: (required)
valid: a self-relative security descriptor
applies: new requests

Who may use this template. Enrol (0x1) permits requests; Auto-enrol (0x2) is
reserved for automatic enrolment.

Enrolment alone grants nothing: every requested name must also be permitted
by its RequestSecurity. The shipped templates let any authenticated
principal enrol, O:SYG:SYD:(A;;0x1;;;AU)(A;;0x3;;;BA)(A;;0x3;;;SY).

--- machine\software\pki\names
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Names

Who may request certificates for which names. Dns holds host names;
Principals holds user@domain names, for email and UPN certificates.

Names are matched case-insensitively and keyed in lowercase. A name's key
lists its labels from the right: radius.lab.home is Dns\home\lab\radius. A
principal name's key lists its domain's labels from the right, then the user
after an @: jack@home is Principals\home\@jack.

A name without its own key, or whose key has no RequestSecurity, receives
the descriptor of the nearest key above it, by ordinary inheritance: only
entries marked to inherit (CI) pass down, at each level. A wildcard name,
*.lab.home, is checked on lab.home and needs Wildcard as well as one of the
request rights. Wildcards are never granted through PRINCIPAL SELF.

--- machine\software\pki\names\dns
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Names\Dns

The root of the host-name tree. Its RequestSecurity is required, so every
host name has a descriptor.

--- machine\software\pki\names\principals
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Names\Principals

The root of the principal-name tree. Its RequestSecurity is required, so
every user@domain name has a descriptor.

--- machine\software\pki\names\<tree>\<name> requestsecurity
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Names\<tree>\<name> RequestSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: inherited from the nearest key above
valid: a self-relative security descriptor; required on Dns and Principals
applies: new requests

Who may request certificates for this name and, through inheritable
entries, the names below it. Request (0x1) permits issuing without
approval. Request with approval (0x2) holds the request for an officer.
Wildcard (0x4) also permits *. this name.

PRINCIPAL SELF (PS) matches the name's Owner, so a grant to PS lets each
owner request its own name. Both roots ship with
O:SYG:SYD:(A;CI;0x1;;;PS)(A;CI;0x7;;;BA)(A;CI;0x7;;;SY): owners may request
their own names, Administrators and SYSTEM may request any name, and nobody
else may request anything.

An explicit value replaces what this name would otherwise inherit; it is
not merged with the descriptors above it. The pki tools include the
inherited entries when they write one. A direct edit must do the same, or
the entries above, such as the grants to Administrators, no longer apply
to this name and the names below it.

Granting Request to a broad group, such as Authenticated Users, lets every
member obtain certificates for those names without approval. Prefer
Request with approval for groups.

--- machine\software\pki\names\<tree>\<name> owner
canonical: Machine\Software\Pki\Names\<tree>\<name> Owner
type: REG_SZ
default: none
valid: a SID in string form
applies: new requests

The principal that owns exactly this name. It is what PRINCIPAL SELF
matches, and certificates for names it owns carry its SID. Ownership doesn't
extend to names below this one. A group may own a name, which makes every
member its owner; never make a broad group, such as Users, an owner.
